==================================================
29. CONFIGURATION
==================================================

Never hardcode secrets.

Use .env:

POSTGRES_HOST=
POSTGRES_PORT=
POSTGRES_DB=
POSTGRES_USER=
POSTGRES_PASSWORD=

EXASOL_DSN=
EXASOL_USER=
EXASOL_PASSWORD=

QDRANT_URL=
QDRANT_API_KEY=

OPENAI_API_KEY=
OPENAI_MODEL=

APP_ENV=
LOG_LEVEL=

Provide .env.example.

==================================================
30. DOCKER
==================================================

Create Docker Compose for local development.

At minimum:

- backend
- frontend
- postgres
- qdrant

Exasol may be external because local Exasol setup can be environment-specific.

Make the application support:
- real Exasol connection
- development fallback if Exasol is unavailable

IMPORTANT:
Do not silently replace Exasol permanently with PostgreSQL.

The code should maintain a clear Exasol analytical adapter.

==================================================
31. EXASOL ADAPTER
==================================================

Create a dedicated Exasol repository/adapter.

The system should be able to run queries such as:

SELECT COUNT(*)
FROM orders;

SELECT COUNT(*)
FROM orders
WHERE customer_id IS NULL;

SELECT order_date, SUM(total)
FROM orders
GROUP BY order_date;

Use parameterized queries where possible.

Do not let arbitrary agent-generated SQL bypass the control layer.

==================================================
32. POSTGRESQL ADAPTER
==================================================

Use SQLAlchemy.

Create:
- models
- repositories
- migrations or initialization scripts

Repositories should isolate persistence from business logic.

==================================================
33. LLM DESIGN
==================================================

Use structured outputs.

Do not depend on free-form LLM text for critical workflow decisions.

LLM should primarily perform:
- reasoning
- investigation synthesis
- root cause hypotheses
- remediation proposal
- explanation

Deterministic code should perform:
- policy checks
- SQL safety
- execution
- verification
- risk scoring
- deployment
- rollback
- final status

==================================================
34. PROMPT DESIGN
==================================================

Create dedicated prompts for:

Investigator:
- analyze incident
- use supplied evidence
- do not invent facts
- identify root cause
- state uncertainty

Engineer:
- produce safe remediation
- prefer minimal change
- explain assumptions
- specify rollback
- never request unrestricted production access

Verifier:
- interpret verification results
- explain whether fix succeeded
- never override deterministic failed checks

==================================================
35. ERROR HANDLING
==================================================

Implement:
- retries for transient failures
- timeout handling
- structured errors
- workflow recovery
- circuit breakers
- safe failure

If an agent fails:
- do not deploy
- preserve incident state
- log error
- expose incident as requiring human attention

==================================================
36. IDEMPOTENCY
==================================================

Important.

A remediation should be idempotent whenever possible.

Running the same fix twice should not corrupt the data.

Implement idempotency checks for:
- incident processing
- deployment
- remediation
- rollback

==================================================
37. CONCURRENCY
==================================================

Prevent two remediation workflows from simultaneously modifying the same dataset.

Implement a basic locking mechanism in PostgreSQL.

For example:
- incident/table-level lock
- deployment lock

==================================================
38. SECURITY
==================================================

Implement:
- environment-based secrets
- least privilege
- SQL allowlists
- audit logs
- no raw secrets in logs
- input validation
- API validation
- production write restrictions

Do not expose database credentials through API responses.

==================================================
39. LOGGING
==================================================

Use structured logging.

Every log should contain relevant IDs:

- incident_id
- workflow_id
- execution_id
- agent
- action
- timestamp
- status

==================================================
40. TESTING
==================================================

Write meaningful tests.

UNIT TESTS:
- risk engine
- SQL validator
- policy engine
- quality checks
- blast radius calculation
- rollback logic

INTEGRATION TESTS:
- FastAPI
- PostgreSQL
- Qdrant
- workflow

END-TO-END:
SCN-02
SCN-03
at minimum.

Test:
- detection
- investigation
- fix generation
- control
- sandbox
- verification
- risk
- approval
- deployment
- rollback
- memory

